# BuildersEye Cline Rules

## Working style

- Autonomous execution is the default for approved tasks. Do not pause for routine approval.
- At the start of a substantial task, inspect `git status`, current branch, and remote. Create
  a recoverable checkpoint before editing:
  1. preserve existing user changes;
  2. create a timestamped backup branch from the current HEAD when possible;
  3. create a task branch for the implementation;
  4. commit the pre-task checkpoint when there are relevant existing changes;
  5. push the backup/task branch when authenticated GitHub access is available.
- Never work directly on `main` for a substantial change unless the user explicitly asks for it.
- The task branch must be pushed so the user can review, compare, or roll back from GitHub.
- Use clear branch names such as `codex/debug-rag-inspector-YYYYMMDD` and
  `codex/backup-before-debug-rag-YYYYMMDD-HHmm`.
- When the user explicitly requests autonomous execution, use Act mode directly and do not
  pause for approval between implementation steps. Create and follow an internal plan.
- Use Plan mode only when the user asks for planning/review before implementation.
- Before editing, inspect the real production code path and identify the files involved.
- Make whatever repository changes are necessary to complete the approved task, including
  refactoring, moving, creating, or deleting obsolete project files when justified.
- Do not stop merely because a file appears only indirectly related; follow the dependency
  chain and complete the work end-to-end.
- Do not ask the user to approve routine file reads, edits, installs, local server starts,
  test runs, or local cleanup that is required by the approved task.
- Preserve unrelated user changes when practical, but prioritize completing the approved task.
- Never reset the repository or run broad destructive commands such as `git reset --hard`.
- Never delete the backup branch or checkpoint commit during the task.
- Keep responses and agent summaries concise: findings, files changed, tests, and blockers.
- Do not repeat full file contents in responses.

## Token and context efficiency

- Read only files relevant to the current task.
- Do not read `node_modules/`, `.git/`, logs, screenshots, archives, generated reports,
  `.xlsx`, `.zip`, or large data files unless directly required.
- Prefer targeted search with `rg` over scanning the whole repository.
- Use summaries between team members instead of passing full source files.
- Avoid concurrent edits to the same file when practical. If overlap is required, have the
  coordinator reconcile the final version.
- Assign teams a primary scope, but allow them to edit supporting files needed to finish their
  work.
- Run targeted tests after each logical group of changes instead of rerunning the full suite
  after every small edit.
- Use high reasoning for architecture/security decisions and medium reasoning for simple
  syntax fixes, formatting, and test execution.
- When context becomes large, summarize completed work before continuing.

## Security

- Never print, expose, commit, or hardcode API keys, passwords, JWT secrets, database URLs,
  refresh tokens, or other credentials.
- Do not copy secrets into documentation, test output, screenshots, or reports.
- Keep test credentials local-only and ensure production-safe defaults.
- Treat client-side gates as UI convenience only, never as real authorization.
- Validate authentication and authorization on the server.
- Redact sensitive values from logs and error messages.

## External systems and deployment

- When the approved task requires an end-to-end working result, you may use the available
  authenticated CLI/tools to commit, push, deploy, and verify changes on GitHub, Vercel,
  Azure Container Apps, Neon, or other project services.
- Do not pause to ask for approval for routine deployment steps that are explicitly part of
  the current task.
- Before deployment, run the relevant local build and focused tests, inspect the diff, and
  confirm that no secret, log, screenshot, or unrelated file is included.
- Deploy only the project changes required by the current task. Do not change unrelated
  infrastructure or data.
- Prefer a preview/staging deployment from the task branch first when the hosting setup
  supports it. Deploy to production only when the approved task explicitly requires the
  production URL to be fixed.
- Production environment changes are allowed when required to make the approved feature work.
  Use existing secret references and environment mechanisms; never print secret values.
- Secret rotation is allowed only when the user explicitly includes rotation in the task.
  Perform it without displaying the new or old secret values, then verify service health.
- If an external service is unavailable, continue all local work and report the exact blocker.

## Testing and verification

- Do not claim a test or deployment passed unless it was actually executed and passed.
- Prefer deterministic tests that do not require an external LLM key.
- NEVER run destructive tests (DELETE, DROP) against the default data directory. Always set a temporary ACCESS_DATA_DIR for smoke tests.
- If an LLM-dependent test cannot run, mark it as skipped and explain why.
- Verify changed JavaScript with syntax checks and run the narrowest relevant test first.
- Check imports and the actual runtime path; do not test stale or unused modules as if they
  were production code.
- Include exact commands and results in the final summary.
- If the user asks for a production fix, verify the deployed URL/API after deployment instead
  of stopping after a local build.

## Repository-specific guidance

- The active backend code is primarily in the flat `server/*.js` files. Verify imports before
  editing anything under `server/core/`, `server/security/`, `server/controllers/`, or
  `server/services/`, because some of those paths may contain stale code.
- The frontend uses Vite with root-level HTML entry points and `src/` JavaScript/CSS.
- Keep the existing Vercel frontend, Azure Container Apps backend, and Neon Postgres design
  compatible unless the task explicitly requests an architectural change.
- Preserve trace node names and ensure documentation matches the actual chat pipeline.
- Remove obsolete code, generated artifacts, stale documentation, or unsafe demo files when
  that is necessary for the approved task. Do not delete broad directories or irreplaceable
  user data without first verifying the target.

## Final response format

Always report:

1. Short result summary.
2. Files changed.
3. Tests actually run and their results.
4. Tests skipped and the reason.
5. Remaining risks or blockers.
6. Whether external deployment is still required.
7. Current branch, backup branch, task branch, checkpoint commit, and pushed remote status.

## STRICT ITIL LOGGING RULE (MANDATORY LOOP)
คุณ (AI) ต้องปฏิบัติตาม **"กฎเหล็ก"** นี้ทุกครั้งเมื่อมีการแก้ไขระบบ แก้บั๊ก หรือแก้ไขเหตุขัดข้องเสร็จสิ้น เพื่อไม่ให้ลืมอัปเดตหรือทำพลาดแบบเดิมซ้ำ (Continuous Loop)
เมื่อทำงานใดๆ เสร็จ คุณ **ต้องอัปเดตตาราง** ในไฟล์ต่อไปนี้เสมอ:

1. **`INCIDENT_LOG.md`**: บันทึกเหตุการณ์ที่ทำให้ระบบใช้งานไม่ได้ และวิธีการแก้ไขเฉพาะหน้าเพื่อให้ระบบกลับมาใช้งานได้ไวที่สุด (Workaround/Quick Fix)
2. **`PROBLEM_LOG.md`**: บันทึกว่าปัญหาเกิดตอนไหน, เกิดจากสาเหตุอะไร (Root Cause), ใครเป็นคนทำ (Who), และวิธีป้องกันไม่ให้เกิดซ้ำ
3. **`CHANGE_LOG.md`**: บันทึกการเปลี่ยนแปลงทุกครั้ง ว่าแก้โค้ดหรือระบบส่วนไหนไปบ้าง และใครเป็นผู้ทำ

**ข้อควรระวัง:** 
- ห้ามจบงาน ปิด Task หรือ Deploy ขึ้น Production จนกว่าไฟล์ทั้ง 3 จะถูกอัปเดตและ Commit เรียบร้อยแล้ว
- ทั้ง 3 ไฟล์ต้องอยู่ในรูปแบบ **ตาราง (Table)** ตามที่โครงสร้างไฟล์ระบุไว้เสมอ
